  {
    "id": "copd-breathing-exercises-home",
    "title": "Easy Breathing Exercises for COPD at Home",
    "summary": "Simple daily techniques that help your loved one breathe easier, panic less, and stay active at home.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Watching someone you love struggle for breath is frightening. The good news: a few simple exercises, done daily, can make breathing easier and reduce that panic.\n\n## Why breathing exercises help\n\nIn COPD, air gets trapped in the lungs. The right techniques push stale air out and bring fresh air in - without exhausting the patient.\n\nThey also calm the mind. Less panic means less breathlessness.\n\n## 1. Pursed-Lip Breathing\n\nThe most useful one. Use it during breathlessness or climbing stairs.\n\n1. Breathe in slowly through the nose for 2 counts.\n2. Purse the lips like blowing out a candle.\n3. Breathe out gently for 4 counts.\n\n> Out should be longer than in. This keeps airways open.\n\n## 2. Belly (Diaphragm) Breathing\n\n- Sit relaxed or lie down.\n- One hand on the chest, one on the belly.\n- Breathe in so the **belly** rises, not the chest.\n- Breathe out slowly. Belly falls.\n\nDo this 5 minutes, twice a day.\n\n## 3. Gentle Daily Movement\n\nStill bodies get weaker. Encourage:\n\n- Short, slow walks inside the home.\n- Arm raises while seated.\n- Standing up and sitting down a few times.\n\nRest whenever needed. Slow and steady wins.\n\n## When to stop and get help\n\nCall for urgent help if your loved one has:\n\n- Lips or fingertips turning blue.\n- Breathlessness even at rest.\n- Confusion or unusual drowsiness.\n- Chest pain.\n\n## A Delhi reality\n\nOn high-pollution days, keep windows shut, use a clean indoor space, and avoid outdoor walks. Pollution worsens COPD fast.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEach calm breath you help them take is a small victory - and they add up."
  },
  {
    "id": "home-oxygen-therapy-safety",
    "title": "Using Oxygen at Home: A Safety Guide",
    "summary": "If a concentrator or cylinder has come home, here is how to use it safely and confidently.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "When oxygen equipment arrives home, it can feel intimidating. Take a breath - with a few rules, it becomes simple and safe.\n\n## Rule one: oxygen and fire do not mix\n\nOxygen makes things burn faster. This is the most important safety point.\n\n- No smoking anywhere near the patient or device.\n- Keep away from gas stove, diya, agarbatti, candles, matchsticks.\n- No oil-based creams on the nose - use water-based gel.\n\n## Setting up the concentrator\n\n1. Place it in an open spot, away from walls and curtains (it needs air).\n2. Keep it on a stable surface, not on carpet that blocks vents.\n3. Set the flow **exactly** as the doctor advised - never increase it yourself.\n4. Check the tubing is not bent or pressed.\n\n## Caring for the patient\n\n- Clean the nasal prongs daily with mild soap and water.\n- Watch for soreness behind the ears or on the nose; pad gently with soft cloth.\n- Keep the patient's mouth and lips moist with water-based gel.\n\n## Power and backup\n\nDelhi power cuts are real. A concentrator needs electricity.\n\n- Keep a backup cylinder if the doctor recommends one.\n- Know how long the backup lasts.\n- Save your Housepital and emergency numbers on speed dial.\n\n## Red flags - get help fast\n\n- Breathing stays hard even on oxygen.\n- Lips, face, or nails turn blue or grey.\n- Severe headache, confusion, or drowsiness.\n- The device alarms and will not reset.\n\n> Never change the oxygen flow rate on your own. More is not always safer.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYou learned something new and important today - that quiet competence is exactly what your loved one needs."
  },
  {
    "id": "asthma-diet-triggers-india",
    "title": "Asthma: Foods That Help and Triggers to Avoid",
    "summary": "Smart, everyday food choices and home changes that help keep asthma attacks away.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Caring for someone with asthma means thinking about more than just the inhaler. What they eat and breathe at home matters too.\n\n## Foods that support the lungs\n\nNo food cures asthma, but some help reduce inflammation.\n\n- **Colourful fruit and veg** - orange, papaya, gajar, palak, tomato.\n- **Vitamin D sources** - eggs, fortified milk, a little morning sun.\n- **Warm fluids** - water, soups, tulsi or ginger tea ease congestion.\n- **Omega-rich foods** - walnuts, flaxseed (alsi), fish if non-veg.\n\n## Foods that may trigger\n\nTriggers differ for each person. Watch for these common ones:\n\n- Very cold drinks and ice cream.\n- Heavily fried, oily, or fast food.\n- Packaged foods with strong preservatives.\n- Any food they are personally allergic to.\n\n> Keep a small diary. Note what was eaten before an attack. Patterns appear.\n\n## Home triggers beyond food\n\n- **Dust** - wash bedsheets weekly in hot water.\n- **Agarbatti and dhoop smoke** - keep away from the patient.\n- **Cold weather** - cover the nose and mouth with a scarf.\n- **Pollution** - on bad AQI days, keep windows shut.\n\n## Stay hydrated\n\nThin mucus is easier to clear. Encourage water through the day, especially in dry Delhi winters.\n\n## When it is an emergency\n\nUse the prescribed reliever inhaler and seek urgent help if:\n\n- The inhaler is not working.\n- They cannot speak full sentences.\n- Lips or nails turn blue.\n- The chest pulls in sharply with each breath.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nSmall daily choices in your kitchen and home add up to easier breathing - and you are already making them."
  },
  {
    "id": "what-is-copd-explained-simply",
    "title": "What Is COPD? A Simple Explainer for Families",
    "summary": "Plain-language answers about the lung condition your loved one is living with - no jargon.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "If the doctor said COPD and you nodded without fully understanding, you are not alone. Let us make it clear and simple.\n\n## What COPD means\n\nCOPD stands for Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease. In plain words: the airways are narrowed and damaged, so air gets trapped and breathing becomes hard.\n\nIt is long-term, but it **can be managed well** with the right care.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nThe biggest causes in India:\n\n- Smoking (including bidi and hookah).\n- Years of breathing smoke from chulha or wood fires.\n- Heavy air pollution.\n- Repeated lung infections in the past.\n\n## Signs to recognise\n\n- A cough that will not go away.\n- Breathlessness on walking or climbing stairs.\n- Wheezing - a whistling sound while breathing.\n- More mucus or phlegm than usual.\n\nThese often get slowly worse over months and years.\n\n## What a flare-up is\n\nSometimes symptoms suddenly worsen - more cough, more breathlessness, coloured phlegm. This is called an exacerbation, or flare-up.\n\n> Catching a flare-up early and calling the doctor can prevent a hospital stay.\n\n## How it is managed\n\n- Inhalers and medicines, taken exactly as prescribed by your doctor.\n- Breathing exercises.\n- Avoiding smoke and pollution.\n- Staying active within limits.\n- Yearly flu protection, if the doctor advises.\n\n## The emotional side\n\nBreathlessness causes fear and frustration. Your loved one may feel low or dependent. Your calm presence matters as much as the medicine.\n\n## Get urgent help if\n\n- Breathing is very difficult, even at rest.\n- Lips or nails turn blue.\n- They become confused or very drowsy.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nUnderstanding the condition is the first step to controlling it - and you have just taken it."
  },
  {
    "id": "pneumonia-recovery-myths-busted",
    "title": "Post-Pneumonia Recovery: Myths vs Facts",
    "summary": "Old beliefs can slow recovery after pneumonia. Here is what is true and what to ignore.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Your loved one is home after pneumonia, and everyone has advice. Let us clear up the myths so recovery stays on track.\n\n## Myth 1: Once the fever goes, they are fully fine.\n\n**Fact:** The lungs take weeks to heal fully. Tiredness and mild cough can linger. Rushing back to full activity too soon can cause a setback.\n\nLet recovery be gradual.\n\n## Myth 2: Cold foods and curd cause pneumonia to return.\n\n**Fact:** Curd and normal foods do not cause pneumonia. It is caused by infection, not by temperature of food. A balanced diet actually speeds healing.\n\n## Myth 3: Bathing will make the chest infection worse.\n\n**Fact:** A warm bath is fine and keeps the patient comfortable and clean. Just keep the room warm and dry them quickly afterwards.\n\n## Myth 4: Stop the antibiotics once they feel better.\n\n**Fact:** This is dangerous. Stopping early lets the infection return stronger. Always finish the full course exactly as prescribed by your doctor.\n\n## Myth 5: Lying in bed all day is the best rest.\n\n**Fact:** Too much lying still allows mucus to settle in the lungs. Gentle sitting up, short walks, and deep breaths help the lungs clear.\n\n> Rest the body, but keep the lungs gently working.\n\n## What actually helps recovery\n\n- Finish all prescribed medicine.\n- Deep-breathing exercises a few times a day.\n- Warm fluids and a protein-rich diet - dal, eggs, paneer, milk.\n- Plenty of sleep and a smoke-free room.\n\n## Watch for warning signs\n\nCall the doctor urgently if you see:\n\n- Fever returning after improving.\n- Breathlessness getting worse.\n- Chest pain or blue lips.\n- Coughing up blood.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nWith patience and the right care, lungs heal well - you are helping them get there safely."
  },
  {
    "id": "stroke-home-recovery-diet",
    "title": "Eating Right After a Stroke: A Home Diet Guide",
    "summary": "Simple, everyday Indian meals that help your loved one recover from a stroke and stay safe while eating.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 4,
    "body": "Caring for someone after a stroke can feel overwhelming, especially at mealtimes. The right food helps healing, controls blood pressure, and lowers the chance of another stroke.\n\n## Why diet matters after a stroke\n\nA stroke often comes with high BP, diabetes, or high cholesterol. Food is one of the strongest tools you have at home to keep these in check.\n\nGood eating also rebuilds strength and supports the brain as it heals.\n\n## What to put on the plate\n\n- **More vegetables and fruit** - palak, lauki, gajar, papaya, apple, orange.\n- **Whole grains** - roti, brown rice, oats, daliya instead of maida.\n- **Good protein** - dal, sprouts, paneer, eggs, curd, soya.\n- **Healthy fats** - a little mustard or olive oil, a few nuts.\n\n## What to limit\n\n- Extra salt, achaar, papad, namkeen - these raise BP.\n- Deep-fried foods, ghee-heavy sweets, full-cream milk.\n- Packaged and processed snacks.\n\n> Cook with less salt for the whole family. It is easier than cooking separately, and good for everyone.\n\n## If swallowing is difficult\n\nMany stroke patients struggle to swallow. This is serious - food can enter the lungs.\n\n1. Keep them sitting upright while eating and for 30 minutes after.\n2. Offer soft foods - khichdi, mashed dal-rice, dalia, curd, banana.\n3. Make liquids thicker if thin water causes coughing.\n4. Feed slowly, small spoonfuls, no rushing.\n\n**Call for urgent help if** they cough or choke badly, turn blue, or food keeps going down the wrong way.\n\n## Easy day plan\n\n- **Morning:** soft daliya or oats with fruit.\n- **Lunch:** soft roti or rice, dal, well-cooked sabzi, curd.\n- **Evening:** fruit or a handful of soaked nuts.\n- **Dinner:** khichdi or vegetable soup with soft rice.\n\nKeep water within reach all day. Dehydration is common and easy to miss.\n\nProgress may be slow. Some days they eat well, some days less. That is normal - keep going gently.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYou are doing one of the kindest things possible - feeding someone back to strength, one meal at a time."
  },
  {
    "id": "dementia-daily-management-home",
    "title": "Managing Dementia at Home: Day-to-Day Help",
    "summary": "Practical routines and calm responses that make daily life easier for a parent with dementia - and for you.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 4,
    "body": "Looking after a parent with dementia tests your patience and your heart every single day. Small changes to the daily routine can reduce confusion and keep them calm.\n\n## What is happening\n\nDementia slowly affects memory, thinking, and mood. Your loved one is not being difficult on purpose - their brain is changing. Remembering this helps you respond with kindness.\n\n## Build a steady routine\n\nSameness brings comfort. Confusion grows when the day feels unpredictable.\n\n- Wake, eat, and sleep at the same times daily.\n- Keep familiar objects in the same place.\n- Do calming activities - tea together, bhajan, a short walk - at fixed times.\n\n## Make the home safe\n\n1. Remove loose rugs and clutter that cause falls.\n2. Lock away medicines, sharp tools, and matchsticks.\n3. Put a name and phone number in their pocket in case they wander.\n4. Use night lights to reduce evening confusion.\n\n## Handle difficult moments\n\nThey may repeat questions, get angry, or accuse family members. Try not to argue or correct harshly.\n\n- Speak slowly, in short sentences.\n- Use their name and a warm tone.\n- Distract gently - offer food, music, or a photo.\n- Step away for a minute if you feel frustrated. That is okay.\n\n> You cannot reason them out of confusion. You can only meet them where they are, with calm.\n\n## Watch for sundowning\n\nMany patients become restless in the late afternoon and evening. Keep that time quiet - dim lights, less noise, a familiar face nearby.\n\n## When to seek help\n\nCall your doctor if you notice:\n\n- Sudden worsening of confusion (this can mean infection).\n- New fever, poor urine, or refusal to eat or drink.\n- Aggression that puts them or others at risk.\n\n## Care for yourself too\n\nCaregiver burnout is real. You cannot pour from an empty cup.\n\n- Share duties with family. Ask for help - it is not weakness.\n- Take short breaks. Even 15 minutes helps.\n- Talk to someone about how you feel.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour patience, even on the hard days, is a quiet form of love they still feel."
  },
  {
    "id": "epilepsy-seizure-myths-busted",
    "title": "Epilepsy Myths That Put Patients at Risk",
    "summary": "Popular beliefs about seizures can do real harm. Here is the truth, so you can respond the right way.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "If someone in your family has seizures, you have probably heard plenty of advice from relatives and neighbours. Some of it is dangerous. Let us separate myth from fact.\n\n## Myth 1: Put a spoon or shoe in their mouth during a seizure.\n\n**Fact:** Never put anything in the mouth. It can break teeth, block breathing, or hurt you. A person cannot swallow their tongue.\n\nInstead, turn them gently on their side and protect their head with something soft.\n\n## Myth 2: Make them smell a shoe or onion to stop the fit.\n\n**Fact:** This does nothing. A seizure stops on its own, usually within 1-2 minutes. Just keep them safe and wait calmly.\n\n## Myth 3: Epilepsy is caused by black magic or past sins.\n\n**Fact:** Epilepsy is a medical condition. It comes from changes in the brain's electrical activity - not from spirits, curses, or anything the family did wrong.\n\nIt can be controlled well with proper treatment.\n\n## Myth 4: Once seizures stop, you can stop the medicine.\n\n**Fact:** Stopping medicine suddenly is one of the biggest causes of dangerous seizures. The medicine is working *because* they take it daily.\n\nNever stop or change it without the doctor's advice.\n\n## Myth 5: People with epilepsy cannot study, work, or marry.\n\n**Fact:** Most people with epilepsy live full, normal lives - school, jobs, marriage, children. Good treatment makes this possible.\n\n> The goal is not to fear epilepsy, but to manage it calmly and correctly.\n\n## What to actually do during a seizure\n\n1. Stay calm. Note the time.\n2. Move sharp or hard objects away.\n3. Turn them on their side.\n4. Put something soft under the head.\n5. Do **not** hold them down or force the mouth open.\n\n**Call emergency help if:** the seizure lasts more than 5 minutes, one seizure follows another, they do not wake up, or they are injured or struggling to breathe.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nKnowing the right response turns a frightening moment into one you can handle with confidence."
